Kartikeya
Description: The stone sculpture depicts the god Kartikeya seated in ardhaparyankasana.
The left leg is folded behind the neck of the bird.
Its body is shown in profile though the fan-like tail is spread out behind the deity's back.
The god wears his hair in the kakapaksa style, a cakra and a makara kundala in the ears, a beaded necklace with pendant armlet case and tiger-claw charms, an upavita, and armlets.
The right-hand holds the shaft of a short heavy spear, while the left-hand strokes the bird's neck.
The lower cloth is marked with ridged folds.
Naga Bust
Description: The sculpture fragment of the Naga image is showing a bust part.
The figure is badly damaged; the two arms and the body below the waist are missing.
Only a part of the snake canopy survives on the left side of the head.
The face is full and heavy, but the eyes are downcast and meditative.
The coiffure consists of curled rolls of hair and a knot on the crown of the head.
The necklace is composed of large graduated beads.
Description: This is a sculpture of four armed Vishnu.
Three of his hands and face is lost.
Bust of Chamunda
